@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title codex paradigm — ML blackbox algorithm (spec → plan → verify loop)

actor "Developer" as DEV

node "codex container" as CTR <<host>> {
  component "defs/codex/entrypoint.sh" as EP <<app>>
  component "codex --dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox" as CLI <<app>>

  frame "Instruction tiers (lowest first)" as TIERS <<host>> {
    component "$CODEX_HOME/AGENTS.md\nproveo house rules" as HOUSE <<app>>
    component "AGENTS.md in the repo\nread natively" as PROJ <<app>>
  }

  frame "In-loop review gates" as GATES <<host>> {
    component "adversarial-reviewer\nsecurity-reviewer\n(sandbox_mode = read-only)" as RO <<app>>
    component "spec-keeper\n(workspace-write — the single writer)" as SK <<app>>
  }

  database "/app — repo" as WS <<db>>
}

node "Layer 2 — container + egress" as L2 <<host>> {
  component "hardened container\ncap-drop · no-new-privileges · pids-limit" as SBX <<app>>
  component "egress tier\nopen · allowlist · review" as EG <<app>>
}

cloud "api.openai.com" as VENDOR <<cloud>>

DEV ARROW_MAIN CLI
CLI ARROW_MAIN HOUSE
HOUSE ARROW_MAIN PROJ : errorLabel(project outranks house)
CLI ARROW_MAIN RO : final diff, before "done"
RO ARROW_ERROR CLI : errorLabel([BLOCKER]/[HIGH] block completion)
SK ARROW_MAIN WS : _spec/ · PLAN.md · AGENTS.md only
CLI ARROW_MAIN WS
CTR ARROW_MAIN SBX
SBX ARROW_CLOUD VENDOR : via EG

note right of TIERS
  **codex has no managed-policy tier, so the house rules sit at the
  BOTTOM — and that is the honest place for them.**

  claudecode bakes proveo's conventions to
  ""/etc/claude-code/CLAUDE.md"", a tier that loads before everything
  else and cannot be excluded at any settings layer. codex has no
  equivalent path. What it has instead is native ""AGENTS.md"": the
  repo's own file is read with no bridge written into the operator's
  checkout, and it ranks ABOVE the global one.

  So the house rules ADD to a repository's rules rather than
  competing with them, and a project can overrule the house. Writing
  them into the workspace to win the ranking was rejected: it mutates
  the operator's tree, and cannot apply at all where an ""AGENTS.md""
  already exists.
end note

note bottom of GATES
  **The read-only split is what makes a review gate a gate.**

  This harness launches with approvals off, so an advisor that could
  edit would be able to fix its own findings and mark itself green.

  codex exposes no per-tool allowlist — there is no ""tools:"" line to
  write — so the control is each agent's ""sandbox_mode"", set to
  ""read-only"". It is the same guarantee claudecode gets from its
  frontmatter, expressed in the only vocabulary codex enforces.
  ""spec-keeper"" is the single writer, scoped by its prompt to
  ""_spec/"", ""PLAN.md"" and ""AGENTS.md"".
end note

note bottom of CLI
  **The CLI is installed system-wide from npm, and pinnable.**
  ""@openai/codex"" ships the same native binary as the install script,
  but a bare ""curl | sh"" resolves whatever release is current, which
  is not a thing an image can reproduce. ""--codex-version"" pins it.

  **Evidence is limited by the CLI, not by policy.** The TUI has no
  verbosity switch; the only dial is ""codex exec --json"", which emits
  each event as JSONL. A caller's own ""--json"" is a parse contract and
  wins. So verbose here buys structured events in headless runs and
  nothing at all interactively — which the preamble says out loud
  rather than implying it bought something.
end note

note bottom of L2
  **The container is the sandbox, and the egress tier is the network
  boundary.** Unlike cursor there is no vendor TLS pin in the way:
  ""api.openai.com"" is broker-injectable, so every egress tier applies
  and the manifest constrains only the provider (""providers: [openai]"").

  Unlike cursor's Landlock, codex's own sandbox COULD run here. It is
  off because it confines the agent to part of a tree that is already
  a disposable copy while its prompts stall an unattended loop —
  a cost with no matching benefit, not an impossibility.
end note

@enduml
